    "body": "## Bug\n\nRaising a tenant's `max_events_per_second` doesn't change the Postgres Changes limit while its `ReplicationPoller` is running. The update stops the `{:channel, :db_events, tenant_id}` `RateCounter`, and the poller starts it again with the limit it read in `init/1`. The poller keeps dropping changes at the old threshold.\n\nThis is the Postgres Changes counterpart of #2274, which #2275 fixed for channels. #2274 mentions this case as a follow-up.\n\n## Deterministic reproduction\n\nIn `test/extensions/postgres_cdc_rls/replication_poller_test.exs` (`describe \"poll\"`, tenant at `max_events_per_second: 123`):\n\n1. Start the poller and let it poll once. The `db_events` counter has `limit.value == 123`.\n2. Call `Realtime.Api.update_tenant_by_external_id(tenant_id, %{\"max_events_per_second\" => 1_000})`. `maybe_restart_rate_counters/1` publishes `:update` for `db_events_per_second_key/1`, and the counter stops. Its cache entry expires one second later.\n3. Have the next poll return a change. The poller calls `RateCounter.get(state.rate_counter_args)`.\n\n## Expected\n\nThe restarted counter enforces `limit.value == 1_000`.\n\n## Actual\n\nThe restarted counter has `limit.value == 123`.\n\n## Root cause\n\n`ReplicationPoller.init/1` builds `rate_counter_args = Tenants.db_events_per_second_rate(tenant)` once and keeps it in state. `RateCounter.get/1` starts a missing counter with the args it's given. Since #1479/#1584, the poller is the only process that gets or starts the `db_events` counter, so after the restart from #1669 it's always the poller that brings the counter back, and always with the init-time limit. A `max_events_per_second` change doesn't restart the poller: only `extensions`, `jwt_secret`, `jwt_jwks` and `suspend` do. The new limit therefore only applies after the poller restarts for some other reason.\n\n## Impact\n\nWhen a tenant that's hitting `MessagePerSecondRateLimitReached: Too many postgres changes messages per second` gets a higher limit, the poller keeps skipping changes (`[:realtime, :replication, :poller, :changes, :skip]` with `reason: :rate_limited`) at the old threshold. The slot has already consumed those changes, so they're never delivered. Lowering the limit is also ignored, so the poller keeps sending at the old, higher rate.\n",
